####################################################################
# inference.ttl — HAND-AUTHORED.  NOT GENERATED.
#
# Expert-curation axioms on the 2_READOUT set (see FOUNDATIONS.md §1, §4).
# Still generic/existential, same claim-kind as kg.ttl/vocab.ttl — no
# particular, no patient, no timestamp belongs in this file.
#
# TWO LAYERS OF AXIOM, AND WHY THE FIRST IS SHAPED DIFFERENTLY
# -------------------------------------------------------------- 
# §BRIDGE axioms cross from the operational instance level to the universal
#   level. They are written as class-level value restrictions:
#
#       metric:SpO2 rdfs:subClassOf [ owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
#                                     owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:SaO2 ]
#
#   This form is REQUIRED, not cosmetic. A plain triple
#   'metric:SpO2 mda:approximates physiologicalProperty:SaO2' treats the
#   concept as an individual (punning); an operational node typed
#   'a metric:SpO2' then reaches NOTHING through it under OWL-RL. With the
#   restriction form (OWL-RL rule cls-hv1), every instance of the metric
#   inherits the link and no duplication into kg.ttl or vocab.ttl is needed.
#   Two chains need a bridge here: Metric -> PhysiologicalProperty, and
#   Device -> TherapeuticModality (see Technical axioms below) — everything
#   past either bridge relates universals to universals.
#
# §TAIL axioms relate universals to universals — property to process,
#   process to organ, organ to organ system. Once the bridge has been
#   crossed, the subject is a concept used as an individual, so plain
#   triples are correct and sufficient. Only ONE bridge is needed per
#   chain; the tail traverses from there.
#
# Reasoning over (situational graph ∪ these axioms) with OWL-RL therefore
# derives the full clinical and therapeutic context of every alarm without
# any alarm archetype carrying a copy of it.
####################################################################

@prefix mda:        <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#> .
@prefix owl:        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:       <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

@prefix device:      <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/device/> .
@prefix component:   <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/component/> .
@prefix opstate:     <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/operation-state/> .
@prefix therapeuticModality: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/therapeutic-modality/> .
@prefix functionalUnit: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/functional-unit/> .
@prefix sensor:      <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/sensor/> .
@prefix signal:      <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/signal/> .
@prefix signalAnalysis: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/signal-analysis/> .
@prefix metric:      <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric/> .
@prefix metricValueState: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric-value-state/> .
@prefix metricRhythm: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric-rhythm/> .
@prefix qualitystate: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/quality-state/> .
@prefix physiologicalProperty: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/physiological-property/> .
@prefix physiologicalProcess:  <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/physiological-process/> .
@prefix organ:       <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/organ/> .
@prefix organSystem: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/organ-system/> .


####################################################################
# Technical axioms
####################################################################

# Device-category -> modality mapping. PLAIN REFERENCE TRIPLES, NOT
# RESTRICTIONS — deliberately, same reasoning and same shape as the
# FunctionalUnit recipes below (see that section's header): a plain triple
# on a punned concept (device:X used here as an individual) does not
# propagate to any per-alarm instance under OWL-RL, so this sits inertly as
# pure lookup data. build_framework.py's build_administers_map() reads it
# directly (mda:administers as subject_objects, no reasoning) and its
# consumer, recipe_override(), mints every alarm on one of these device
# categories its own individuated TherapeuticModality blank node and a real
# per-row mda:administers edge — using the CSV's own TherapeuticModality
# value when given, this mapping otherwise.
#
# This used to be five class-level owl:Restriction axioms instead (device:X
# rdfs:subClassOf [administers hasValue Y]) — changed to plain triples for
# the same reason the FunctionalUnit recipes are plain triples and not
# restrictions, but with a sharper consequence here: mda:TherapeuticModality
# switched from Referential to Individuated (see its nodeKind comment in
# ontology.ttl) specifically so mda:hasTherapyDeliveryQuality (Therapy
# delivery quality axioms, below) could be derived per-alarm. With the
# class-level restriction still in place, a device would end up
# administering BOTH the shared concept (via cls-hv1 on its type) AND its
# own per-alarm modality node (via the per-row edge) at the same time — and
# hasOperationState/hasTherapyDeliveryQuality's owl:propertyChainAxiom
# (ontology.ttl) propagates through EVERY administers edge it finds,
# without discriminating, contaminating the shared concept with per-alarm
# state that isn't true for every other alarm referencing it. Confirmed:
# hasOperationState had this exact leak silently, from before this file's
# TherapeuticModality individuation work — device:MechanicalVentilator-typed
# alarms with Malfunction/Disconnected states were entailing that value onto
# the shared therapeuticModality:VentilationTherapy concept globally. Plain
# triples read only by build_framework.py, never loaded into a reasoner's
# entailment path the restriction shape was, remove that second target
# entirely — nothing is left to leak through, for either property.
device:Dialysator                       mda:administers therapeuticModality:RenalReplacementTherapy .
device:ExtracorporealMembraneOxygenator mda:administers therapeuticModality:ExtraCorporealMembraneOxygenation .
device:Incubator                        mda:administers therapeuticModality:ThermoRegulationTherapy .
device:Thermoregulator                  mda:administers therapeuticModality:ThermoRegulationTherapy .
device:MechanicalVentilator             mda:administers therapeuticModality:VentilationTherapy .


# The broad device categories are mutually exclusive — a device miscategorised
# into two of these (e.g. both Incubator and Thermoregulator) is a data error,
# not a legitimate multiple-inheritance case, so a reasoner should be able to
# catch it as an inconsistency. Hand-authored here, not in ontology.ttl,
# because these are vocabulary-level concepts (2_READOUT-generated, punned as
# classes via rdfs:subClassOf mda:Device). Deliberately only the BROAD
# categories, not the auto-generated manufacturer-precoordinated leaves
# under them (e.g. device:PhysiologicalMonitor_Philips) — those are generated
# per (broad class, manufacturer) pair by build_framework.py's precoordinate(),
# so disjointness among sibling leaves would need to come from the generator
# itself, not a static list here that would go stale the moment a new
# manufacturer is added.
[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( device:Dialysator device:ExtracorporealMembraneOxygenator
                 device:Incubator device:Thermoregulator
                 device:MechanicalVentilator device:PhysiologicalMonitor
                 device:CardiacMonitor ) .


####################################################################
# FunctionalUnit recipes
#
# The internal technical wiring of a functional unit — which sensor(s) it
# reads, which signal(s) those produce, which analysis step(s) consume
# those signals, which metric(s) each step produces — is never CSV/kg
# data (DATA/ANNOTATION/consensus/p75_annotated.csv only carries
# FunctionalUnit + Metric; no Sensor/Signal for these). It is expert
# knowledge, hand-authored here once per FunctionalUnit concept.
#
# PLAIN REFERENCE TRIPLES, NOT RESTRICTIONS — deliberately, unlike the
# Technical/Clinical axioms elsewhere in this file. Every hop below,
# including the first (FunctionalUnit -hasSensor-> Sensor), used to be
# written the bridge-once way (an owl:hasValue restriction on the
# FunctionalUnit class, cls-hv1), which forces the value onto EVERY
# instance of that class unconditionally. That was wrong: it made a
# functional unit's full technical "menu" — which sensor(s) it is capable
# of reading — indistinguishable from what a given alarm actually
# evidences. FU_InvasiveBloodPressure genuinely has two independent,
# separately-pluggable lines (arterial, venous); an alarm about one of them
# does not mean the other is connected. The same is true even where a
# single sensor really does feed several simultaneous analyses
# (FU_PulseOximetry's one PPG waveform -> SpO2 + PulseRate + PulseFlowIndex
# all at once, physically) — an alarm naming only SpO2 tells us nothing about
# whether PulseRate or PulseFlowIndex were normal, abnormal, or even being
# looked at for that event, so asserting them anyway overclaims exactly the
# same way. So: no restriction fires this chain onto any per-alarm
# instance at all now. This is purely a lookup table — CODE/framework_build/
# build_framework.py's build_metric_recipe() reads it directly, walking
# FunctionalUnit -hasSensor-> Sensor -sensorProducesSignal-> Signal
# -analyzedBy-> SignalAnalysis -producesMetric-> Metric forward, and asserts,
# per CSV row, only the single trace that row's own FunctionalUnit + Metric
# columns actually name. See build_framework.py's module docstring /
# recipe_override() for the generation side.
#
# A functional unit that produces more than one metric (e.g. FU_PulseOximetry
# -> SpO2, Pulse, PulseFlowIndex) fans out at the mda:analyzedBy hop: one
# shared signal, several mda:SignalAnalysis steps, each with its own
# mda:producesMetric. A functional unit with genuinely distinct sensors
# (FU_InvasiveBloodPressure's arterial and venous lines) instead gets two
# separate mda:hasSensor triples, each with its own downstream chain. Either
# shape works the same way at grounding time: the lookup is keyed by
# (FunctionalUnit, Metric), so it always resolves to exactly one Sensor/
# Signal/SignalAnalysis regardless of how many siblings that trace has.
#
# KEYED ON THE PAIR, NOT ON METRIC ALONE — changed during the 2026-09
# annotation curation round. A metric name is not unique across functional
# units in the p75 corpus: metric:RespirationRate is produced both here
# (FU_PneumoTachograph, flow-derived, 8 alarms) and by impedance
# pneumography under FU_ElectroCardiography (3 alarms), and
# metric:VentilationCircuitLeak appears under both FU_PneumoTachograph and
# FU_AirwayPressure. Under the old metric-only key, build_metric_recipe()'s
# dict comprehension silently kept whichever trace happened to parse last
# and handed it to every alarm naming that metric, regardless of which
# functional unit the alarm's own row names. The pair is a complete key:
# all 17 (FunctionalUnit, Metric) combinations in the p75 corpus are
# distinct. A row naming a Metric with no FunctionalUnit gets no recipe at
# all now rather than a guessed one — deliberately visible as a generic
# mda:Sensor/mda:Signal node rather than silently wrong.
#
# mda:hasSensor's own owl:InverseFunctionalProperty characteristic was
# removed earlier (see its comment in 1_BASE/ontology.ttl) — the collision
# risk that motivated never repeating a sensor concept across two
# FunctionalUnit types no longer applies, but sensor names still don't
# repeat here, simply because each is a genuinely distinct physical part.
#
# ── Trace: Electrocardiography sensing pathway ─────────────────────────
#
# Expanded to the real instrument in the 2026-09 curation round. Source:
# Paul A, Jacob J. "Electrocardiographic lead reversals" (2023), PMC10685096.
#
# TEN ELECTRODES, TWELVE LEADS — not twelve sensors. The leads are DERIVED
# measurements, which is why they are modelled as mda:Signal produced by an
# mda:SignalAnalysis rather than as sensors or as metrics:
#
#   * a lead is a voltage trace over time, which is what a Signal is; a
#     Metric in this framework is the quantity an alarm reports with a value
#     state, and no alarm is ever "about aVR"
#   * making leads Metrics would leave metric:HeartRate — which every ECG
#     alarm actually names — with no producing analysis
#   * mda:producesSignal's union domain (Sensor OR SignalAnalysis) exists
#     for exactly this, and its comment already names "augmented lead aVF,
#     combined from other leads" as the case it was written for
#
# Layers: electrode -> raw signal -> derivation -> lead signal ->
# interpretation -> metric.
#
# sensor:ECG_RL_sensor is the neutral/ground electrode. It is modelled
# because it is a real electrode that can come loose, but it produces no
# raw signal and contributes to no lead — per the source, "the right leg
# electrode serves as the neutral".
#
# THREE OR FIVE ELECTRODES IS A SUBSET OF THIS, NOT A DIFFERENT MAPPING.
# Continuous ICU monitoring typically places 3 or 5 electrodes rather than
# all 10; the derivations below are unchanged, a smaller set simply yields
# fewer leads. Nothing here asserts that all ten are present on any given
# alarm — see the per-alarm note at the end of this section.

functionalUnit:FU_ElectroCardiography mda:hasSensor
    sensor:ECG_RA_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_LA_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_LL_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_RL_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_V1_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_V2_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_V3_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_V4_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_V5_sensor,
    sensor:ECG_V6_sensor .

## Electrode -> raw signal (RL is ground: no signal, no lead)
sensor:ECG_RA_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_RA_signal .
sensor:ECG_LA_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_LA_signal .
sensor:ECG_LL_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_LL_signal .
sensor:ECG_V1_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_V1_signal .
sensor:ECG_V2_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_V2_signal .
sensor:ECG_V3_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_V3_signal .
sensor:ECG_V4_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_V4_signal .
sensor:ECG_V5_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_V5_signal .
sensor:ECG_V6_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ECG_V6_signal .

## Derivation -> lead signal. Formulas from the source, verbatim in intent:
##   I = LA - RA      II = LL - RA      III = LL - LA
##   aVR = RA - (LA+LL)/2   aVL = LA - (RA+LL)/2   aVF = LL - (LA+RA)/2
##   Vn  = Vn - Wilson's central terminal, WCT = (RA+LA+LL)/3
## The dependency asserted is which raw signals each derivation INGESTS,
## not the arithmetic — OWL carries no formulas. Every augmented and
## every precordial lead therefore depends on all three limb electrodes,
## the precordials because WCT is their reference.

signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_I .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_I .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_I mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_I .

signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_II .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_II .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_II mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_II .

signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_III .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_III .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_III mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_III .

signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVR .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVR .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVR .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVR mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_aVR .

signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVL .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVL .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVL .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVL mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_aVL .

signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVF .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVF .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVF .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_aVF mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_aVF .

signal:ECG_V1_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V1 .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V1 .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V1 .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V1 .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V1 mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_V1 .

signal:ECG_V2_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V2 .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V2 .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V2 .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V2 .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V2 mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_V2 .

signal:ECG_V3_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V3 .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V3 .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V3 .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V3 .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V3 mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_V3 .

signal:ECG_V4_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V4 .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V4 .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V4 .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V4 .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V4 mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_V4 .

signal:ECG_V5_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V5 .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V5 .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V5 .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V5 .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V5 mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_V5 .

signal:ECG_V6_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V6 .
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V6 .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V6 .
signal:ECG_LL_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V6 .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_V6 mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_V6 .

## Interpretation -> metric. HeartRateAnalysis ingests the RHYTHM LEADS,
## not the whole twelve. Continuous monitoring derives rate and arrhythmia
## from a designated primary lead with a secondary for confirmation, II and
## III conventionally; the precordials and augmented leads exist for
## morphology (ST segment, axis), which no alarm in this corpus reports. A
## future metric such as ST elevation collapses onto its own leads the same
## way, by adding its own analysis here — that is what this layer is for.
##
## Narrowing this is what lets the SIGNAL slot of an ECG heart-rate alarm
## resolve to something real instead of the ontology's bare mda:Signal.
## Which of the two leads a given alarm used is still not determined, so
## that slot lands on their shared supertype (signal:ECG_signal) rather than
## on one of them — see build_metric_recipe()/recipe_override().
signal:ECG_lead_II mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:HeartRateAnalysis .
signal:ECG_lead_III mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:HeartRateAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:HeartRateAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:HeartRate .

## GROUP TYPES — the supertype every ECG electrode and every ECG signal
## shares. These are not new concepts: they are the two the researchers were
## already annotating with (sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor on "ECG
## afleidingen los" / "ECG alle leads los", signal:ECG_signal on "ECG
## artefact"), given a formal place rather than left denoting a whole with
## no relation to its parts.
##
## Their job is to keep a per-alarm node HONEST when the trace is not
## determined. Before this, an ECG heart-rate alarm asserted "hasSensor [ a
## mda:Sensor ]" — the ontology's top sensor class, which a pulse oximeter
## satisfies just as well as an electrode. Saying "some ECG electrode"
## claims exactly what the alarm does evidence and nothing more.
##
## Expressed as subtyping rather than as a SensorGroup entity with a
## part-of property: a group here is a KIND of sensor, not a thing that has
## members, so no new class or property is needed and existing queries over
## mda:Sensor keep working unchanged.
sensor:ECG_RA_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_LA_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_LL_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_RL_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_V1_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_V2_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_V3_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_V4_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_V5_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_V6_sensor rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .
sensor:ECG_lead_Resp rdfs:subClassOf sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor .

signal:ECG_RA_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_LA_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_LL_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_V1_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_V2_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_V3_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_V4_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_V5_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_V6_signal rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_I rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_II rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_III rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_aVR rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_aVL rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_aVF rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_V1 rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_V2 rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_V3 rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_V4 rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_V5 rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_V6 rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .
signal:ECG_lead_Impedance rdfs:subClassOf signal:ECG_signal .

## Impedance pneumography reuses the RA and LA electrodes, measuring
## transthoracic impedance across them rather than potential.
signal:ECG_RA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_Impedance .
signal:ECG_LA_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_Impedance .
signalAnalysis:ECG_derivation_Impedance mda:producesSignal signal:ECG_lead_Impedance .
signal:ECG_lead_Impedance mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ImpedanceRespirationAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:ImpedanceRespirationAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:RespirationRate .

# PER-ALARM CONSEQUENCE, and the reason build_metric_recipe() now resolves
# to a SET: metric:HeartRate is reachable from nine different electrodes
# here, so no heart-rate alarm determines which one it evidences. The
# builder asserts a trace only where the set is a singleton, and otherwise
# stops at the FunctionalUnit — see that function's docstring. Naming one
# electrode on a high-heart-rate alarm would be a fabrication, not a
# simplification. Contrast FU_InvasiveBloodPressure, whose two lines are
# genuine ALTERNATIVES: there, an alarm naming one metric does pick out one
# trace. The ECG lead set is not alternatives; it is one instrument.


####################################################################

## Trace: Pulse oximetry sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_PulseOximetry mda:hasSensor sensor:PulseOximeter .
sensor:PulseOximeter mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:PPG_waveform .
signal:PPG_waveform mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:SpO2Analysis, signalAnalysis:PulseAnalysis,
                                    signalAnalysis:PulseFlowIndexAnalysis .

signalAnalysis:SpO2Analysis           mda:producesMetric metric:SpO2 .
signalAnalysis:PulseAnalysis          mda:producesMetric metric:PulseRate .
signalAnalysis:PulseFlowIndexAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:PulseFlowIndex .

## Trace: Invasive blood pressure sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_InvasiveBloodPressure mda:hasSensor sensor:ABP_transducer, sensor:VenousPressureTransducer .
sensor:ABP_transducer mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:ABP_waveform .
signal:ABP_waveform    mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:ArterialPressureAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:ArterialPressureAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:ArterialBloodPressure .

sensor:VenousPressureTransducer mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:Pressure_waveform .
signal:Pressure_waveform         mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:VenousPressureAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:VenousPressureAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:VenousBloodPressure .

## Trace: Airway pressure sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_AirwayPressure mda:hasSensor sensor:AW_transducer .

sensor:AW_transducer mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:AirwayPressure_waveform .
signal:AirwayPressure_waveform mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:AirwayPressureAnalysis,
                                              signalAnalysis:CPAPAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:AirwayPressureAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:AirwayPressure .

# CPAPAnalysis added in the 2026-09 curation round, from Nena's HULBUS -
# CPAP high / CPAP low comments ("sensor is leeg: moet deze niet AW
# transducer zijn?"). She is right: continuous positive airway pressure is
# read off the same airway-pressure transducer and the same waveform as
# peak pressure — it is a second analysis of one signal, which is the
# fan-out shape this section's header already describes for
# FU_PulseOximetry. Before this, (FU_AirwayPressure, CPAP) had no recipe,
# so both alarms fell through to a generic mda:Sensor/mda:Signal node.
#
# SETTLED: metric:CPAP stays its own metric, and is deliberately NOT collapsed
# into metric:AirwayPressure at a baseline phase. It is physically the same
# baseline airway pressure that SERVOU - PEEP laag annotates as
# metric:AirwayPressure + metricPhase:EndExpiratory, but the two arise under
# different ventilation modes (spontaneous vs mandatory) and the annotator
# call is that the mode distinction is clinically real. The consequence to be
# aware of: HULBUS and SERVOU baseline-pressure alarms are therefore NOT
# cross-manufacturer equivalent under CQ7, by choice rather than by oversight.
signalAnalysis:CPAPAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:CPAP .

## Trace: Ventilation volume sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_PneumoTachograph mda:hasSensor sensor:PneumoTachograph .

sensor:PneumoTachograph mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:AirwayFlow_waveform .

signal:AirwayFlow_waveform mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:VolumeAnalysis,
                                          signalAnalysis:RespiratoryRateAnalysis,
                                          signalAnalysis:LeakAnalysis .

signalAnalysis:VolumeAnalysis          mda:producesMetric metric:RespirationVolume .
signalAnalysis:RespiratoryRateAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:RespirationRate .

# LeakAnalysis added in the 2026-09 curation round. metric:VentilationCircuit
# Leak used to be annotated under TWO functional units — FU_PneumoTachograph
# on the two Dräger alarms, FU_AirwayPressure on HULBUS - Leak too high and
# SERVOU - Te veel lekkage — which is what forced the recipe key onto
# (FunctionalUnit, Metric) in the first place. Settled as an annotation slip,
# not a second real pathway: a circuit leak is the difference between
# inspired and expired volume, so it needs a flow sensor, and both devices
# demonstrably have one (HULBUS - Vte high and SERVOU - Exp. minuutvol. both
# annotate FU_PneumoTachograph on the same hardware). The two rows were
# corrected in p75_annotated.csv and the metric now has exactly one trace.
#
# The (FunctionalUnit, Metric) key stays regardless: metric:RespirationRate
# is still genuinely produced by two pathways (this one and impedance
# pneumography under FU_ElectroCardiography), so the pair is still the only
# key that resolves it.
signalAnalysis:LeakAnalysis            mda:producesMetric metric:VentilationCircuitLeak .

## Trace : Capnography sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_Capnograph mda:hasSensor sensor:Capnometer .

sensor:Capnometer mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:Capnogram_waveform .
signal:Capnogram_waveform mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:CO2Analysis .
signalAnalysis:CO2Analysis mda:producesMetric metric:CO2 .

## Trace: Humidity sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_Humidity mda:hasSensor sensor:Humidity_sensor .

sensor:Humidity_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:Humidity_signal .
signal:Humidity_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:HumidityAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:HumidityAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:Humidity .

## Trace: Temperature sensing pathway
functionalUnit:FU_Temperature mda:hasSensor sensor:Temperature_sensor .

sensor:Temperature_sensor mda:sensorProducesSignal signal:Temp_signal .
signal:Temp_signal mda:analyzedBy signalAnalysis:TemperatureAnalysis .
signalAnalysis:TemperatureAnalysis mda:producesMetric metric:AirTemperature .


####################################################################
# Component operational-state propagation
#
# mda:hasOperationState's propertyChainAxiom (1_BASE/ontology.ttl) no longer
# includes mda:hasComponent — that link was a blanket rule firing for EVERY
# component of EVERY device, unable to distinguish a component whose failure
# means the device itself is unavailable (ConnectionUnit: the data link is
# gone; PowerUnit: the power is gone) from one that doesn't (MattrasHeater/
# RadiantHeater/System: a peripheral subsystem warning, not a device-wide
# fact). 1_BASE/ontology.ttl's mda:CriticalComponent names exactly the
# former group as a TBox class (a plain vocabulary-free structural
# distinction, so IT belongs there); the single rule below fires on that one
# class, and the two plain triples after it tag which vocabulary-level
# component concepts from this catalogue actually count as critical. The
# rule itself has to live here rather than in ontology.ttl even though the
# class doesn't: its hasValue target (opstate:Disconnected) is a
# 2_READOUT-generated vocabulary concept, the same reason every other axiom
# in this file can't move either.
#
# Shape: the class of devices that hasComponent some (CriticalComponent,
# with this operation state) — an owl:someValuesFrom restriction nested
# inside the outer intersection, not a bridge-then-tail chain like the
# clinical axioms below, because the fact being reached (the DEVICE's own
# state) lives on a different node than the one the restriction is written
# on (the COMPONENT). Confirmed firing correctly and precisely (a
# Disconnected ConnectionUnit's device entails Disconnected; a Warning
# MattrasHeater's device entails nothing) with an isolated reasoner test
# before writing this — re-confirmed after collapsing the original two
# component-type-specific copies of this axiom into this one general form.
####################################################################

[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Device
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasComponent ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:CriticalComponent
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasComponentOperationState ;
              owl:hasValue opstate:Disconnected ] ) ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasDeviceOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Disconnected ] .

component:ConnectionUnit rdfs:subClassOf mda:CriticalComponent .
component:PowerUnit      rdfs:subClassOf mda:CriticalComponent .


####################################################################
# Signal quality axioms
#
# The first axiom below was keyed on metric:Pulse until the 2026-09
# annotation curation round, and had therefore never fired: no archetype
# carries that concept. The alarm it was written for — PHILIPSMONITOR -
# SpO2 geen pols — carries metric:PulseRate, the name the rest of the
# framework adopted in the same update that renamed the recipes (see the
# FunctionalUnit recipes header and EVALUATION/CQs/temporal/
# CQ10_coinciding_equivalence.rq's own record of that rename). Its sibling
# axiom below it, on metric:PulseFlowIndex, was unaffected and has been
# firing all along, which is why CAT1a's validation pair never surfaced
# this: that pair exercises an asserted Signal.QualityState row
# (PHILIPSMONITOR - Storing in SpO2), not either entailed branch.
#
# hasQualityState's domain is mda:Signal, and Signal is never CSV-asserted
# for a Metric-only row (see FunctionalUnit recipes above) — it only exists
# per-alarm as the anonymous connective blank node 2_READOUT/build_framework.py
# mechanically inserts between Sensor and SignalAnalysis while resolving
# Metric's ontology-tree ancestors.
#
# Each axiom below walks FORWARD along the pipeline's own declared
# properties — Signal -analyzedBy-> SignalAnalysis -producesMetric-> Metric,
# nested owl:someValuesFrom restrictions, one per hop — from the class of
# Signals down to the rate-qualified metric, rather than deriving a
# metric-to-signal property to walk backward across (mda:correlatesWithSignal,
# removed: it closed a real loop in the property graph — Signal ->
# SignalAnalysis -> Metric -> back to Signal — for no benefit over reaching
# the same signal by walking the already-declared forward edges). Confirmed
# equivalent behaviour with an isolated reasoner test before rewriting:
# fires correctly (cls-svf2 promotes SignalAnalysis and then Signal into the
# nested intersection classes, cls-hv1 then fires the hasValue restriction),
# and stays scoped to only the triggering alarm's own per-alarm blank nodes
# — a second, unrelated alarm reaching the same metric TYPE through its own
# separate chain sees nothing. That scoping is what matters here, not which
# direction the walk happens to run: it holds either way, because in both
# versions the restriction is only ever satisfied by an individual's OWN
# structural neighbours, never by a shared concept.
####################################################################

[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Signal
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:analyzedBy ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:SignalAnalysis
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:producesMetric ;
              owl:someValuesFrom
                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( metric:PulseRate
                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasRhythm ; owl:hasValue metricRhythm:Absent ] ) ] ] ) ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasQualityState ; owl:hasValue qualitystate:Impaired ] .

[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Signal
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:analyzedBy ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:SignalAnalysis
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:producesMetric ;
              owl:someValuesFrom
                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( metric:PulseFlowIndex
                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasValueState ; owl:hasValue metricValueState:Decreased ] ) ] ] ) ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasQualityState ; owl:hasValue qualitystate:Impaired ] .


####################################################################
# Sensor baseline operational-state axiom
#
# A Metric-bearing archetype (2_READOUT/p75_annotated.csv rows that name a
# Metric but no Sensor/Component/Device operational state of their own — 62
# of 101 archetypes, checked directly against the corpus before writing
# this) currently asserts NOTHING for mda:hasOperationState's
# persistsPostAlarm window to carry: the sensor pathway that produced the
# reading has no operational-state fact at all, so once its alarm ends it
# vanishes from background_at immediately, not after the post-alarm window
# — see CODE/evaluation_poc/mda_poc_assessment.py's CAT1b comment for the
# concrete consequence (an IBP sensor that just alarmed with a plain
# measurement value could not stay structurally known even one tick past
# its own alarm's end).
#
# The axiom: a Sensor whose signal is analyzed to produce ANY Metric is
# thereby known to have been functioning enough to produce that reading —
# not an overclaim about anything the alarm doesn't evidence (contrast the
# FunctionalUnit recipes section's warning above, which blocks inferring
# about a SIBLING metric/sensor the alarm says nothing about; this only
# claims something about the exact pathway that fired). Same walk-forward
# shape as the Signal quality axioms directly above, just landing on
# mda:hasSensorOperationState instead of mda:hasQualityState, and with no
# hasValueState restriction on the Metric — firing for the metric's mere
# existence, not a specific rate value.
#
# Deliberately ONE axiom, not one per node kind (Sensor/FunctionalUnit/
# Device): mda:hasOperationState's own owl:propertyChainAxiom (ontology.ttl)
# already propagates a sub-property state upward through hasSensor and
# hasFunctionalUnit, so asserting Enabled on the Sensor alone entails the
# generic mda:hasOperationState Enabled on its owning FunctionalUnit and
# Device for free — confirmed with an isolated reasoner test before writing
# this (a minimal Device->FunctionalUnit->Sensor->Signal->Analysis->Metric
# ABox plus this axiom correctly derived hasOperationState Enabled on all
# three levels, not just the Sensor). A second axiom targeting
# hasDeviceOperationState directly would only duplicate what the existing
# chain already does.
#
# Scope check performed before writing: no archetype in the current corpus
# has BOTH a Metric AND an explicit Sensor/Component/Device operational
# state of its own (the 62 Metric-bearing and 31 operational-state-bearing
# archetypes are currently fully disjoint sets) — so this cannot currently
# contradict a real annotated state. A future archetype that annotates both
# would need re-checking against this axiom's interaction, since
# mda:hasSensorOperationState is not declared functional here and a
# conflict would surface as two asserted values rather than an error.
#
# No owl:intersectionOf at any hop, unlike the Signal quality axioms above —
# deliberately, not an oversight. Every intermediate hop's type is ALREADY
# guaranteed by rdfs:domain/rdfs:range (sensorProducesSignal is domain
# Sensor/range Signal, analyzedBy is domain Signal/range SignalAnalysis,
# producesMetric is domain SignalAnalysis/range Metric — see their own
# declarations in ontology.ttl), so wrapping each someValuesFrom target in
# an owl:intersectionOf with that same class was asking OWL-RL's cls-int1/
# cls-int2 rules to re-derive class membership the domain/range rules
# (prp-dom/prp-rng) had already established — confirmed identical
# entailment with an isolated reasoner test both ways before adopting this
# form (~20% faster per call; most of a single reason() call's cost is the
# static graph's own fixed re-derivation, not this axiom specifically — see
# reason()'s own docstring). The Signal quality axioms above intersect with
# a SPECIFIC metric subtype (metric:PulseRate, not the domain/range-implied
# mda:Metric), which genuinely isn't implied by any property's range, so
# their intersectionOf is not the same redundancy and is left as-is.
####################################################################

[ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:sensorProducesSignal ;
  owl:someValuesFrom
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:analyzedBy ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:producesMetric ;
          owl:someValuesFrom mda:Metric ] ]
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasSensorOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Enabled ] .


####################################################################
# Structural operational-state propagation
#
# Replaces the producesSignal / hasSensor / hasFunctionalUnit
# owl:propertyChainAxioms that mda:hasOperationState used to carry — see its
# declaration in ontology.ttl for the full reasoning, including the
# MEDIBUS - Humidity Sensor inoperable case that exposed them.
#
# THE CLAIM: a child node reporting ANY operational state is evidence its
# PARENT is operational, because the alarm carrying that state reached us
# through the parent. Not "propagate the good values and suppress the bad
# ones" — the parent's entailed value is always opstate:Enabled, never a
# copy of whatever the child reported. A sensor that is Malfunction,
# Disconnected or Disabled still proves the device it hangs off is powered
# and communicating.
#
# WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT A GUARDED CHAIN: a chain copies the value it finds
# and cannot be conditioned on it. Expressing "only enabling values
# propagate" would need the polarity in a separate sub-property plus one
# pairing axiom per concept, and would still copy the value rather than
# assert Enabled. Writing the rule on the PARENT instead needs neither, and
# is the shape the CriticalComponent rule above and the Metric monitoring
# rule below already use.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT SELF-APPLY: the antecedent tests the CHILD's state and the
# consequent lands on the PARENT, so a device carrying its own
# Disconnected never thereby infers itself Enabled. That failure mode is
# real and was the reason for not routing this through a sub-property of
# hasOperationState with a chain: such a property is entailed onto the node
# itself, re-satisfies its own antecedent, and hands a genuinely dead device
# an Enabled alongside its Disconnected.
#
# A device that IS down says so itself — its own Device.OperationalState
# row, or the CriticalComponent rule above for single-point-of-failure
# parts. Those two paths are untouched.
#
# mda:hasComponent is absent here for exactly the reason it was absent from
# the old chains (see ontology.ttl): a Component may be peripheral, and the
# CriticalComponent rule above is the deliberate, targeted exception.
#
# The Signal -> Sensor level is deliberately NOT written. No corpus row
# annotates an operational state on a Signal (signals carry
# mda:hasQualityState instead), and the Sensor baseline axiom above already
# gives every metric-producing sensor opstate:Enabled, so the rule would be
# inert twice over.
####################################################################

# THE LADDER RUNS THE WHOLE SENSING PATHWAY, one hop per rung:
#
#   Metric          -- opstate:Monitored, from the Metric monitoring axiom
#                      below, whenever the metric carries any of its four
#                      characteristics (value state, phase, aggregation,
#                      rhythm). This is the bottom of the ladder and the
#                      only rung not written here.
#   SignalAnalysis  -- its metric reports something, so the analysis ran
#   Signal          -- its analysis reports something, so the signal flowed
#   Sensor          -- its signal reports something, so the sensor produced
#   FunctionalUnit  -- its sensor reports something
#   Device          -- its functional unit reports something
#
# Each rung looks exactly one hop down and asserts on itself, so the chain
# composes without any rung needing to know the pathway's depth.
#
# VALUE: opstate:Enabled at every rung above Metric, deliberately NOT
# opstate:Active. Active is already in use for a genuinely different sense —
# DATEXOHMEDACOMVENTILATOR - Plimit Reached annotates a
# component:PressureReleaseValve as Active, meaning the valve is currently
# venting, an event rather than an operational status. Overloading it to
# also mean "this signal is flowing" would make the two indistinguishable
# to any query. opstate:Monitored stays specific to Metric, as it already
# was. The resulting reading is consistent: data is Monitored, everything
# that carries or produces it is Enabled.
#
# RELATIONSHIP TO THE SENSOR BASELINE AXIOM ABOVE: that axiom tests the
# whole pathway in one shot (sensor -> signal -> analysis -> some Metric)
# and asserts Enabled on the sensor, skipping the two middle rungs — which
# is exactly why Signal and SignalAnalysis were the only nodes in a
# metric-bearing archetype with no operational state at all before this
# ladder existed (confirmed against MEDIBUS - MV ondergrens and
# PHILIPSMONITOR - SpO2 laag). Both are kept. They currently fire on the
# same archetypes, since every metric-bearing corpus row carries at least
# one metric characteristic and therefore always reaches Monitored — but
# they are not redundant in principle: the baseline still covers a metric
# with no characteristic, and the ladder still covers a signal given a state
# by some route other than its own metric.

# SignalAnalysis whose Metric reports any state  ->  the analysis is Enabled
[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:SignalAnalysis
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:producesMetric ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ;
          owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Enabled ] .

# Signal whose SignalAnalysis reports any state  ->  the signal is Enabled
[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Signal
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:analyzedBy ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ;
          owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Enabled ] .

# Signal reporting any state  ->  its Sensor is Enabled
[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Sensor
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:sensorProducesSignal ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ;
          owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasSensorOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Enabled ] .

# Sensor reporting any state  ->  its FunctionalUnit is Enabled
[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:FunctionalUnit
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasSensor ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ;
          owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Enabled ] .

# FunctionalUnit reporting any state  ->  its Device is Enabled
[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Device
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasFunctionalUnit ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasOperationState ;
          owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasDeviceOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Enabled ] .


####################################################################
# Metric monitoring axiom
#
# Complementary to the Sensor baseline axiom directly above, not a
# duplicate of it: that one concludes something about the SENSOR ("this
# pathway was functioning enough to produce a reading"); this one
# concludes something about the METRIC itself ("this specific measurement
# type is what was being read"). The Sensor-level fact alone cannot
# express that distinction for a Sensor/FunctionalUnit shared by more than
# one metric type (e.g. one ECG-derived FunctionalUnit producing both
# HeartRate and RespirationRate) — knowing the shared pathway is Enabled
# says nothing about which metric it was actively producing at a given
# moment.
#
# Deliberately conditioned on the metric carrying an actual interpretation
# (hasValueState, hasPhase, hasAggregation, or hasRhythm — whichever the
# metric's own kind uses),
# not on mere mda:Metric typing: a bare, uninterpreted Metric node (typed
# but reporting nothing) should not be called "Monitored". Confirmed with
# an isolated reasoner test before writing this (a Metric with hasValueState
# entails Monitored; the same Metric typed but with no hasValueState/hasPhase/hasAggregation/
# hasRhythm at all does not).
#
# opstate:Monitored is declared here, not in FRAMEWORK/VOCABULARY/seed/
# vocab_base.ttl — deliberately. Every other opstate:* value is either a
# CSV-driven generated stub or (for the handful seeded directly) a term
# that exists independently of any one axiom. Monitored is neither: no CSV
# column will ever name it, and it has no meaning apart from this axiom's
# own conclusion. Minimal declaration only (type + scheme membership, no
# hand-authored skos:prefLabel/definition/notation) — this is the axiom's
# by-product, not a curated vocabulary entry.
#
# hasMetricOperationState needs no mda:persistsPostAlarm tag of its own —
# see ontology.ttl's declaration comment: it is rdfs:subPropertyOf
# mda:hasOperationState, which already carries the tag, and
# 4_OPERATIONAL/op_knowledge.py's persisting-state extraction reads the
# entailed general property, the same mechanism the other three
# hasOperationState sub-properties already rely on.
####################################################################

opstate:Monitored a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme opstate:Scheme .

[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Metric
    [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf (
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasValueState  ; owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ]
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasPhase       ; owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ]
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasAggregation ; owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ]
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasRhythm      ; owl:someValuesFrom owl:Thing ]
      ) ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasMetricOperationState ; owl:hasValue opstate:Monitored ] .


####################################################################
# Therapy delivery quality axioms
#
# mda:hasTherapyDeliveryQuality is distinct from the Signal quality axioms
# above (measurement reliability) and from hasOperationState (device
# connectivity/functioning): it is about whether the THERAPY itself is being
# delivered without compromise. Device can be fully connected and working
# while what it delivers to the patient is nonetheless impaired — a
# ventilator circuit leak is exactly this: the device isn't malfunctioning,
# but the ventilation it delivers is compromised.
#
# Same walk-forward-from-the-individuated-node shape as the Signal quality
# axioms, just rooted one class higher: Device -hasFunctionalUnit->
# FunctionalUnit -hasSensor-> Sensor -sensorProducesSignal-> Signal
# -analyzedBy-> SignalAnalysis -producesMetric-> Metric, one nested
# owl:someValuesFrom per hop. Fires only on THIS alarm's own per-alarm blank
# nodes (Device/FunctionalUnit/Sensor/Signal/SignalAnalysis are all
# Individuated/structural — see ontology.ttl's nodeKind comments), same
# scoping guarantee as above.
#
# The device-level conclusion here is then carried on to whichever
# TherapeuticModality this same device administers, declaratively, via
# hasTherapyDeliveryQuality's own owl:propertyChainAxiom in ontology.ttl
# ([inverseOf administers] . hasTherapyDeliveryQuality) — that chain needs a
# real per-alarm mda:administers edge to exist to fire across, which is
# exactly why mda:TherapeuticModality was switched from Referential to
# Individuated (see its nodeKind comment) and why build_framework.py no
# longer suppresses that edge. No procedural per-metric special case needed
# in build_framework.py at all: this is fully reasoner-derived, the same way
# every other clinical/technical axiom in this file is.
####################################################################

# SUBJECT IS THE MODALITY, NOT THE DEVICE — rewritten in the 2026-09
# curation round alongside narrowing mda:hasTherapyDeliveryQuality's domain
# (see its declaration in ontology.ttl for the full reasoning).
#
# This axiom used to be subjected on mda:Device, because the structural
# condition it tests — functional unit, sensor, signal, analysis, leaking
# metric — physically hangs off the device. The value then reached the
# modality only via a propertyChainAxiom, leaving BOTH nodes carrying it.
# The condition still starts at the device; the axiom now walks to it from
# the modality through an inverse-administers hop, so exactly one node ends
# up with the value and it is the one the claim is actually about.
#
# The inverse-property-expression restriction (owl:onProperty [owl:inverseOf
# mda:administers]) is the same shape clinicalEvents.ttl already uses over
# inverse isPropertyOf/approximates. Confirmed firing under this OWL-RL
# stack with an isolated probe before adopting it here: a modality
# administered by a matching device classifies; the device itself does not.
[ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:TherapeuticModality
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:administers ] ;
      owl:someValuesFrom
        [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Device
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasFunctionalUnit ;
              owl:someValuesFrom
                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:FunctionalUnit
                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasSensor ;
                      owl:someValuesFrom
                        [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Sensor
                            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:sensorProducesSignal ;
                              owl:someValuesFrom
                                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:Signal
                                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:analyzedBy ;
                                      owl:someValuesFrom
                                        [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( mda:SignalAnalysis
                                            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:producesMetric ;
                                              owl:someValuesFrom
                                                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( metric:VentilationCircuitLeak
                                                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasValueState ;
                                                      owl:hasValue metricValueState:Increased ] ) ] ] ) ] ] ) ] ] ) ] ] ) ] ] ) ] ] )
] rdfs:subClassOf
  [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasTherapyDeliveryQuality ; owl:hasValue qualitystate:Impaired ] .


####################################################################
# Clinical axioms
####################################################################


metric:SpO2 rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:SaO2 ] .

# ABP and the literal-named "ArterialPressure" metric were two CSV names for
# the same reading — unified in p75_annotated.csv into ArterialBloodPressure,
# so the separate ABP/ArterialPressure bridges (and the ABP_Mean comment
# explaining its inherited-not-duplicated bridge) are retired with them;
# ArterialBloodPressure_Mean inherits from the base below the same way.
metric:ArterialBloodPressure rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:ArterialPressure ] .

# Renamed from VenousPressure in the same unification pass.
metric:VenousBloodPressure rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:VenousPressure ] .

metric:PulseFlowIndex rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:TissuePerfusion ] .

metric:HeartRate rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:ElectricalHeartRate ] .

# Renamed from Pulse in p75_annotated.csv's unification pass — the metric
# now shares its local name with physiologicalProperty:PulseRate (distinct
# namespace, same pattern already used by Temperature/Humidity/
# RespirationVolume below). Still the mechanically-sensed (PPG) beat rate,
# distinct from HeartRate's electrical (ECG) origin — approximates its own
# PulseRate property rather than reusing ElectricalHeartRate, even though
# both trace back to the same cardiac contraction.
metric:PulseRate rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:PulseRate ] .

# CO2Analysis (FunctionalUnit recipes above) produces plain metric:CO2, not
# a phase-refined concept — bridge the base, not a hand-typed "CO2_EndTidal"
# that nothing in the pipeline actually instantiates (its suffix doesn't
# even match a real metricPhase: notation — only EndExpiratory/Mean/Minute/
# Tidal exist). Found while auditing this file: the previous axiom bridged
# a concept the KG generator can never produce, so a CO2 alarm would have
# dead-ended with no approximates link at all.
metric:CO2 rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:PaCO2 ] .

# Renamed from RespiratoryRate — p75_annotated.csv had briefly carried both
# spellings (a genuine typo on one Datex-Ohmeda row, not a distinct
# concept); fixed at the source, so only this one name remains.
metric:RespirationRate rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:RespirationRate ] .

# Renamed from Volume — now shares its local name with
# physiologicalProperty:RespirationVolume (see PulseRate's comment above for
# the pattern). Unphased RespirationVolume bridges here;
# RespirationVolume_Minute/_Tidal need no bridge of their own — same
# reasoning as ABP_Mean had, they inherit this via generated
# rdfs:subClassOf metric:RespirationVolume.
metric:RespirationVolume rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:RespirationVolume ] .

# VentilationCircuitLeak (unified from the former separate Leak/
# VentilationLeak names) is deliberately left unbridged, same as before the
# rename: a circuit/mask-integrity fact, not a patient physiological
# measurement.

# AirwayPressure_EndExpiratory needs no bridge of its own for the same
# reason — inherits this via generated rdfs:subClassOf metric:AirwayPressure.
metric:AirwayPressure rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:RespirationPressure ] .

# CPAP is a delivered airway pressure, same property as AirwayPressure.
metric:CPAP rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:RespirationPressure ] .

metric:AirTemperature rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:Temperature ] .

# Patient body temperature (PhysiologicalMonitor), as opposed to
# AirTemperature's ambient/incubator reading — both approximate the same
# Temperature property today; a future model may want to split them.
metric:Temperature rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:Temperature ] .

metric:Humidity rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:approximates ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:Humidity ] .


# ── therapeutic modality → process ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The therapeutic chain's own tail, completing it the same way the clinical
# chain (below) already was: hand-authored here so it is guaranteed complete
# regardless of what any CSV row happens to name, rather than depending on a
# row co-populating both TherapeuticModality and PhysiologicalProcess (which
# it usually does, but not always — device:Dialysator administers
# therapeuticModality:RenalReplacementTherapy is entailed correctly, but
# before this addition its targetsProcess had no value anywhere: no CSV row
# ever names RenalReplacementTherapy alongside a PhysiologicalProcess, so
# the chain silently dead-ended for exactly that one modality). Audit found,
# not previously deliberate.
#
# RESTRICTIONS, NOT PLAIN TRIPLES — changed in the 2026-09 curation round.
# These were written as plain triples on a punned concept, and were
# therefore INERT: a per-alarm mda:administers node is a blank node typed
# `a therapeuticModality:X`, and OWL-RL has no rule carrying a property from
# a class to its instances, so no alarm ever reached a process through
# them. Confirmed by isolated reasoner test before and after: the shared
# concept reached all three ECMO processes, a node typed with that concept
# reached nothing.
#
# What made this invisible is that the CSV's own PhysiologicalProcess
# column hand-asserts the same edge per row, so the link APPEARED on
# whichever alarms an annotator happened to fill that column in for, and
# was missing on the rest. That is exactly what Rianne asked about on
# CARDIOHELP - pVen onder alarmgrens — "[ECMO]-targetsProcess-[pulmonary
# oxygenation] ontbreekt, wel aanwezig bij pVen onder waarschuwingsgrens
# ... Wat maakt dat -targets process- soms wel verschijnt en soms niet?"
# Two of the four CARDIOHELP rows had the column filled; two did not. The
# answer to her question is that it was never the axiom firing at all.
#
# The hasValue restriction form is the one §BRIDGE prescribes at the top of
# this file and the one mda:approximates already uses throughout the
# clinical tail above: cls-hv1 then gives every instance the edge, per
# alarm, without the CSV needing to repeat it.
#
# This does NOT reintroduce the leak that removed the class-level
# restrictions from the mda:administers section above. That leak was
# specific to administers: a restriction there gave a device a SECOND
# administers edge to the shared concept, and both owl:propertyChainAxioms
# (mda:hasOperationState, mda:hasTherapyDeliveryQuality) traverse
# administers, so per-alarm state propagated onto the shared modality
# concept globally. Neither chain traverses mda:targetsProcess — verified
# against ontology.ttl — and these restrictions point OUTWARD from the
# per-alarm modality node to a Referential process concept, which is the
# same shape mda:approximates has always had. Nothing propagates back.
therapeuticModality:VentilationTherapy rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:targetsProcess ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryVentilation ] .

therapeuticModality:ThermoRegulationTherapy rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:targetsProcess ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProcess:ThermoRegulation ] .
# ECMO targets THREE processes, not one (2026-09 curation round, from
# Rianne's CARDIOHELP - Verbinding verbroken comments):
#
#   PulmonaryOxygenation  the original, unchanged — the membrane oxygenates.
#   PulmonaryDiffusion    the membrane also clears CO2. Rianne suggested
#                         physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryVentilation for this
#                         ("ECMO doet oxygenatie en 'ventilatie' een
#                         diffusie (uitwassen van CO2)"); deliberately NOT
#                         used. Ventilation is bulk movement of gas in and
#                         out of the lungs, which ECMO does not do at all —
#                         what it does is gas exchange across a membrane,
#                         which is diffusion. PulmonaryDiffusion is where
#                         physiologicalProperty:PaCO2 already routes, so an
#                         EtCO2 alarm and an ECMO alarm now converge on one
#                         process, which is the reasoning Rianne was after
#                         (she notes this is relevant to setting lower EtCO2
#                         alarm limits on the ventilator).
#   BloodCirculation      the circulatory support. SCOPE LIMITATION, stated
#                         explicitly: this framework does not distinguish VV
#                         from V(V)A ECMO, which Rianne asked about in the
#                         same comment. VV supports gas exchange only;
#                         V(V)A additionally supports circulation, so this
#                         edge is true only of the latter. It is asserted
#                         unconditionally because the corpus's own
#                         CARDIOHELP - pArt alarms presuppose an arterial
#                         return cannula, i.e. VA or VVA. Splitting the
#                         modality concept in two is the correct fix and is
#                         deferred: it needs a CSV column value per alarm
#                         (the modality is annotator-supplied), not an
#                         axiom here. See FRAMEWORK/CURATION_LOG.md.
therapeuticModality:ExtraCorporealMembraneOxygenation rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:targetsProcess ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryOxygenation ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:targetsProcess ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryDiffusion ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:targetsProcess ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProcess:BloodCirculation ] .
therapeuticModality:RenalReplacementTherapy rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:targetsProcess ;
      owl:hasValue physiologicalProcess:RenalFiltration ] .

# ── property → process ───────────────────────────────────────────
physiologicalProperty:SaO2               mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryOxygenation .
physiologicalProperty:PaCO2              mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryDiffusion .
physiologicalProperty:RespirationRate    mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryVentilation .
physiologicalProperty:RespirationVolume  mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryVentilation .
physiologicalProperty:RespirationPressure mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryVentilation .
physiologicalProperty:ElectricalHeartRate mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction .
physiologicalProperty:PulseRate          mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction .

# SystemicCirculation split into its arterial and venous sides — distinct
# processes with distinct drivers (arteriolar tone/resistance downstream of
# cardiac ejection, vs. venous tone/capacitance and circulating volume
# upstream of cardiac filling), so properties route to whichever side
# actually produces them rather than a single undifferentiated bucket.
#
# ArterialPressure: jointly produced by the heart's pumping action AND
# arterial resistance/tone (MAP ≈ cardiac output × systemic vascular
# resistance) — isPropertyOf is not functional, so both genuinely apply.
physiologicalProperty:ArterialPressure   mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction,
                                                           physiologicalProcess:ArterialCirculation .

# VenousPressure: deliberately NOT tied to CardiacContraction — it's
# governed by venous tone/capacitance and circulating volume, not the
# contraction event itself. That coupling is real but far looser than the
# arterial side's, so asserting it here would overclaim.
physiologicalProperty:VenousPressure     mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:VenousCirculation .

# TissuePerfusion: its only approximating metric (PulseFlowIndex) is
# derived from PPG pulsatility — an arterial-side phenomenon — not the
# textbook arterial-minus-venous perfusion-pressure definition, so it
# routes to ArterialCirculation only, not VenousCirculation too.
physiologicalProperty:TissuePerfusion    mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction,
                                                           physiologicalProcess:ArterialCirculation .
physiologicalProperty:Temperature        mda:isPropertyOf physiologicalProcess:ThermoRegulation .

# ── process → organ ──────────────────────────────────────────────
physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryVentilation  mda:presentIn organ:Lungs .
physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryOxygenation  mda:presentIn organ:Lungs .
physiologicalProcess:PulmonaryDiffusion    mda:presentIn organ:Lungs .
physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction    mda:presentIn organ:Heart .
# ArterialCirculation/VenousCirculation replace the former single
# SystemicCirculation bucket (itself a replacement for VascularCompliance —
# a category error, compliance is a vessel-wall parameter/property, not a
# process). No existing organ concept distinguishes arteries from veins, so
# both sides still presentIn the same organ:BloodVessels — only the process
# split, not the organ granularity; introducing separate Artery/Vein organs
# wasn't asked for and nothing here needs that resolution yet.
physiologicalProcess:ArterialCirculation   mda:presentIn organ:BloodVessels .
physiologicalProcess:VenousCirculation     mda:presentIn organ:BloodVessels .

# BloodCirculation is the whole circulatory loop, of which the arterial and
# venous sides above are the two constituent parts (2026-09 curation round).
# Added because a therapy can act on the entire loop rather than on one leg
# of it — ECMO's arterial return supports circulation as such, not
# specifically the arterial or specifically the venous side — while metrics
# stay routed to whichever side actually produces them (ArterialPressure and
# TissuePerfusion to the arterial leg, VenousPressure to the venous one, all
# unchanged above).
#
# mda:processPartOf, NOT skos:broader/rdfs:subClassOf: see that property's
# own declaration in ontology.ttl for why this is a part-whole claim and not
# a taxonomic one. The practical consequence is the direction of travel —
# an ABP alarm reaches ArterialCirculation from its own metric and then
# BloodCirculation through the part-whole edge, so it converges with an ECMO
# alarm at the whole; an ECMO alarm does NOT thereby claim anything about
# the arterial leg specifically, which is correct (it supports the loop, and
# which leg its cannulae sit in is a VV/VA distinction this framework does
# not yet carry — see the ECMO targetsProcess comment above).
#
# presentIn organ:BloodVessels, matching both its parts. Deliberately NOT
# also organ:Heart, even though the heart drives circulation: CardiacContraction
# already covers the heart's own contribution and is reached independently
# from ElectricalHeartRate/PulseRate, so adding Heart here would merge the
# pump and the plumbing into one undifferentiated node and make every
# circulatory alarm look cardiac. Same "only the process split, not the
# organ granularity" discipline as the comment above.
physiologicalProcess:ArterialCirculation   mda:processPartOf physiologicalProcess:BloodCirculation .
physiologicalProcess:VenousCirculation     mda:processPartOf physiologicalProcess:BloodCirculation .
physiologicalProcess:BloodCirculation      mda:presentIn organ:BloodVessels, organ:Heart .
# RenalFiltration/Kidneys/Renal are newly introduced here, for the
# RenalReplacementTherapy tail above — no prior concept in this vocabulary
# covered renal physiology at all. Worth a domain-expert check on naming.
physiologicalProcess:RenalFiltration       mda:presentIn organ:Kidneys .

# ── organ → organ system ─────────────────────────────────────────
organ:Lungs        mda:organPartOfSystem organSystem:Pulmonary .
organ:Heart        mda:organPartOfSystem organSystem:CardioVascular .
organ:BloodVessels mda:organPartOfSystem organSystem:CardioVascular .
organ:Kidneys      mda:organPartOfSystem organSystem:Renal .

